/-!
# Set (d) of `A₁⁽⁶⁾`: pole cancellation, one constant at a time

Write each truncated master expansion `Lⱼ` of `QED.ThreeLoopD.SetDMastersLaurent` as
`Lⱼ = ε⁻³ ∑_κ κ ℓⱼκ(ε)` over the ten constants
`κ ∈ {1, π², ζ₃, π² ln 2, π⁴, a₄, ln⁴2, π² ln²2, ζ₅, π² ζ₃}`, with `ℓⱼκ` polynomials with
rational coefficients. Bringing the coefficients to the common denominator
`ε ^ pⱼ · setDDen ε` (`QED.ThreeLoopD.setDCoeffIj_eq`), the `κ`-part of the combination is
`P_κ(ε) / (ε⁵ setDDen ε)` with
`P_κ = ∑ⱼ (-1) ^ (lines j) setDAIj · ε ^ (2 - pⱼ) · ℓⱼκ` (`QED.ThreeLoopD.setDP…`).

For each `κ` we prove the polynomial identity
`P_κ(ε) = ε⁵ (v_κ setDDen ε + ε G_κ(ε))` (`QED.ThreeLoopD.setDP…_eq`), with `v_κ` the
`κ`-coefficient of the Jegerlehner–Nyffeler closed form and `G_κ` an explicit polynomial
(`QED.ThreeLoopD.setDG…`). The vanishing of the coefficients of `ε⁰, …, ε⁴` of
`P_κ - ε⁵ v_κ setDDen` is the cancellation of the poles `ε⁻⁵, …, ε⁻¹` in the `κ`-part; the
coefficient of `ε⁵` is its value. Each identity is univariate, with rational coefficients.
The polynomials are generated by a script from `coeffs.json` and the LR96/Laporta table.
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace QED.ThreeLoopD

